Show native views on Windows: the app's composition content under GPUI's swap chain, through DirectComposition.

What: `WindowsWindow::attach_native_view` takes any COM object `IDCompositionVisual::SetContent` takes (usually a
composition swap chain the app presents to on its own device and thread) and returns an attachment whose drop takes
it out again; `native_view_input` is 0048's `Window`, as a DirectComposition visual takes no input. The renderer's
DirectComposition tree becomes, back to front under one root visual: a background visual, a container of one
subtree per native view (a clip visual holding a content visual, after Chromium's `VisualSubtree`), and GPUI's own
swap chain visual. Each frame, after `Present`, `DirectComposition::show` compares the scene's views
(`native_views.rs`: placement in whole device pixels, back-to-front order, background colour) with what the tree
shows, restacks only when the views or their order change, places only the views that moved, and calls
`Commit` only then, so the hole and the views' placement reach the same composition frame and a frame that moves
no view commits nothing (Chromium's `DCLayerTree::CommitAndClearPendingOverlays` order: present, then build and
commit the tree). `PrimitiveBatch::NativeViews` draws a hole: a `native_view` shader writing transparent black with
blending off, at the batch's fixed depth with the depth test on, so opaque quads painted after the view (drawn
first by the opaque pass) keep their pixels and everything painted before it is erased, as Metal's
`native_view_fragment` does. The composition swap chain's alpha mode is one table (`AlphaMode::new`): `Ignore` for an
opaque window with no native view shown, `Premultiplied` otherwise; a change recreates the swap chain, logged at
info, and its first frame is presented before the commit that shows it. `NativeView::set_background`'s colour (black
by default, as on macOS) fills a 1×1 swap chain stretched under the views, shown only for an opaque window while a
view is shown, where a view's content hasn't caught up with its element. A lost device rebuilds every view's
visuals from the content the app gave. The code is in two new files, `native_views.rs` (pure decisions) and
`directx_renderer/native_view_tree.rs` (the visual tree, the hole pipeline, the attachment); `directx_renderer.rs`
and `window.rs` get only hooks (a field, a call per stage, the alpha mode parameter, the shader module row).

Why: issue #1 (NativeView on every desktop). GPUI on Windows already presents through DirectComposition, so the
app's content is one more visual in the same tree: no child window (no airspace problem: GPUI's window keeps all
input), no copy of the app's frames, and the app's presents reach the screen without GPUI drawing or committing
(DWM picks up a composition swap chain's present by itself). Only a frame that moves, shows, hides or restacks a view
costs a `Commit`; an opaque window blends only while it shows a view. Upstream's `PrimitiveBatch::NativeViews` arm was
a no-op. Modelled on Chromium's ui/gl/dc_layer_tree.cc @ 4fc26f32cd03f0e8443c4aa11d6859a901d90659 (BSD-3-Clause,
third_party/chromium/PORTED.md); WebView2's visual hosting (`CreateCoreWebView2CompositionController`) is the same
shape, a visual in the host's tree.

Test: `native_views.rs`'s tables (placement per layout and scale, alpha mode per appearance, frame stacking and
background) run on any host through `scripts/patch-test.sh` (windows-pure). Live: `scripts/windows-native-view-checks.sh`
on CI's Windows runner reads the composed screen back at 100 and 125 %: the hole shows the view, the div over it
covers it, one device pixel past each edge is GPUI's, no gap colour while moving and after a resize; input over the
view reaches its element with positions in the view; 0 GPUI frames per second while only the view presents; the alpha
mode is logged (docs/native-view-windows.md).

Upstream: not filed (patch rule 14).

